<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexandria Eschate</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">329 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Alexandria the Furthest</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> Eschate, 'Alexandria the Furthest', was a city founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> in 329 BCE on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> (Jaxartes) at the entrance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Fergana Valley</a>, at or near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khujand">Khujand</a> in Tajikistan. Intended as a frontier stronghold against the Saka nomads beyond the river, it was the north-easternmost of Alexander's foundations and later lay on routes linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexandria-eschate.jpg" alt="Alexandria Eschate" width="237" height="118" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PHG · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EuthydemusI.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
